    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p29-q16-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why $\\log_{10}300$ lies between 2 and 3.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$10^2<300<10^3$, so $2<\\log_{10}300<3$.",
          "worked": "$$10^2<300<10^3.$$\n\nSince logarithms base 10 are increasing, taking logarithms gives\n\n$$2<\\log_{10}300<3.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q16-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If $x$ is a two-digit number, what two integers does $\\log_{10}x$ lie between?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "1 and 2: $1\\leq\\log_{10}x<2$.",
          "worked": "A two-digit number satisfies $10\\leq x<100$.\n\nTaking logarithms base 10 gives $1\\leq\\log_{10}x<2$. The lower bound is attained when $x=10$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q16-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "If $\\log_{10}x=4.7$, how many digits does $x$ have to the left of the decimal point?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "5 digits.",
          "worked": "$$4<\\log_{10}x<5.$$\n\nConverting to powers of 10 gives $10^4<x<10^5$. Thus $x$ has 5 digits to the left of the decimal point.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q16-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Find $\\log_{10}25^{20}$, and hence find the number of digits in $25^{20}$. Give the logarithm correct to 2 decimal places.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\log_{10}25^{20}\\approx27.96$; 28 digits.\n\nThe logarithm lies between 27 and 28, so the number has 28 digits.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\log_{10}(25^{20})&=20\\log_{10}25\\\\\\log_{10}(25^{20})&\\approx27.96\\end{align*}$$\n\nUsing the unrounded value, $27<\\log_{10}(25^{20})<28$, so $10^{27}<25^{20}<10^{28}$. Therefore $25^{20}$ has 28 digits.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q16-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Find $\\log_{10}2^{1000}$, and hence find the number of digits in $2^{1000}$. Give the logarithm correct to 2 decimal places.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\log_{10}2^{1000}=1000\\log_{10}2\\approx301.03$; 302 digits.\n\nThe logarithm lies between 301 and 302, so the number has 302 digits.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\log_{10}(2^{1000})&=1000\\log_{10}2\\\\\\log_{10}(2^{1000})&\\approx301.03\\end{align*}$$\n\nUsing the unrounded value, $301<\\log_{10}(2^{1000})<302$, so $10^{301}<2^{1000}<10^{302}$. Therefore $2^{1000}$ has 302 digits.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      }
    ]
  },
